CH = dict(num=5, tag="Biology", title="Life Processes", theme="Nutrition, respiration, transport and excretion in plants and animals",
intro="A living body is never idle: even during sleep it feeds, breathes, circulates fluids and removes wastes. Without these maintenance jobs, the body would break down. This chapter follows four sets of life processes: how organisms obtain and use food (nutrition), how they get energy from it (respiration), how materials move around the body (transportation) and how wastes are removed (excretion).",
big_questions=["How do organisms obtain food and convert it into energy?", "How are materials carried to and from every cell?", "How are metabolic wastes removed from plants and animals?"],
blocks=[
("h2", "5.1 What are Life Processes?"),
("p", "Life processes are the basic activities that **maintain** living things. They need energy, raw materials and a mechanism of exchange. Because single cells and even whole bodies are exposed to damage, organisms constantly repair themselves through **nutrition, respiration, transportation and excretion**."),
("h2", "5.2 Nutrition"),
("note", "DEFINITION", "**Nutrition** is the process of obtaining food and using it for energy, growth and repair. **Autotrophs** make their own food from simple inorganic substances; **heterotrophs** depend on other organisms."),
("h3", "Autotrophic nutrition: photosynthesis"),
("note", "FORMULA", "**6CO<sub>2</sub> + 6H<sub>2</sub>O &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>12</sub>O<sub>6</sub> + 6O<sub>2</sub>** in the presence of sunlight and chlorophyll."),
("steps", [("1 — Absorption of light energy by chlorophyll", "Light is trapped by the green pigment in chloroplasts."), ("2 — Splitting of water", "Light energy converts into chemical energy, and water splits into hydrogen and oxygen."), ("3 — Reduction of carbon dioxide", "Carbon dioxide is reduced to carbohydrate; these steps need not take place together.")]),
("ul", ["**Stomata** are tiny pores on leaves, with **guard cells** that control opening. They let CO<sub>2</sub> in and O<sub>2</sub> out; much water vapour also escapes. Stomata close when guard cells lose water.", "Carbon dioxide is taken from air; water and minerals (nitrogen, phosphorus) come from the soil through roots.", "Starch is the stored product; its presence in leaves is tested with iodine.", "Desert plants take CO<sub>2</sub> at night and prepare an intermediate that is used during the day."]),
("h3", "Heterotrophic nutrition"),
("table", ["Mode", "Description", "Examples"], [["Saprophytic", "Break down dead and decaying matter outside the body, then absorb it", "Fungi: bread mould, yeast, mushrooms"], ["Parasitic", "Derive food from a living host without killing it quickly", "Cuscuta, tapeworm, lice, ticks"], ["Holozoic", "Ingest solid food and digest it internally", "Amoeba, humans, dogs"]]),
("p", "**Amoeba** engulfs food with pseudopodia into a food vacuole, digests it with enzymes, absorbs it and expels the leftover. **Paramecium** takes food through a fixed spot by cilia movement."),
("h3", "Nutrition in human beings"),
("table", ["Part", "Function"], [["Mouth", "Teeth chew; **salivary amylase** begins digesting starch to sugar; tongue mixes saliva"], ["Oesophagus", "Peristaltic movement pushes food to the stomach"], ["Stomach", "**HCl** creates acidic medium, kills bacteria and activates **pepsin**, which digests proteins; mucus protects the lining"], ["Small intestine", "Longest part. **Bile** from the liver emulsifies fats and makes food alkaline; pancreatic juice has **trypsin** (proteins), **lipase** (fats), amylase; intestinal juice finishes digestion. **Villi** absorb food"], ["Large intestine", "Absorbs water; the rest is egested through the anus"]]),
("ul", ["Liver is the largest gland; the pancreas secretes enzymes and insulin.", "Dental caries: sugars and bacteria form acid that dissolves enamel; brushing removes plaque."]),
("h2", "5.3 Respiration"),
("note", "DEFINITION", "**Respiration** is the process of breaking down food to release energy, stored in **ATP**. It occurs in the cell, first in cytoplasm (glycolysis, glucose to **pyruvate**) and then in mitochondria (aerobic)."),
("table", ["Pathway", "Where", "Products"], [["Aerobic", "Mitochondria, with oxygen", "CO<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O + **38 ATP** worth of energy"], ["Anaerobic in yeast", "Cytoplasm, without oxygen", "Ethanol + CO<sub>2</sub> + energy"], ["Anaerobic in muscle", "When oxygen is short", "**Lactic acid** + energy; build-up causes cramps"]]),
("note", "FORMULA", "Glucose &rarr; pyruvate (3C) in cytoplasm; pyruvate &rarr; CO<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O + energy (aerobic) | pyruvate &rarr; ethanol + CO<sub>2</sub> (yeast) | pyruvate &rarr; lactic acid (muscle). ATP &rarr; ADP + P releases energy for cell activity (about 30.5 kJ per mole)."),
("ul", ["**Plants:** gas exchange through stomata (leaves), lenticels (stems) and root surface; plants need less exchange as their demand is lower.", "**Humans:** air passes nostrils &rarr; pharynx &rarr; larynx &rarr; trachea (cartilage rings) &rarr; bronchi &rarr; bronchioles &rarr; **alveoli**, thin-walled balloon-like air sacs with rich blood supply; here O<sub>2</sub> enters blood and CO<sub>2</sub> leaves.", "**Haemoglobin** in red blood cells carries oxygen; CO<sub>2</sub> is carried mostly dissolved in plasma. Lung disease or low haemoglobin cause breathlessness.", "Aquatic animals breathe faster because water holds little dissolved oxygen; fish take it with gills."]),
("h2", "5.4 Transportation"),
("h3", "In human beings"),
("p", "The human heart is a muscular four-chambered organ: two **atria** (receive) and two **ventricles** (pump; thicker walls). A septum keeps oxygenated and deoxygenated blood separate, which supports the high energy needs of warm-blooded animals (**double circulation**)."),
("steps", [("Pulmonary circuit", "Right ventricle &rarr; lungs (CO<sub>2</sub> out, O<sub>2</sub> in) &rarr; left atrium."), ("Systemic circuit", "Left ventricle &rarr; body &rarr; right atrium via vena cava."), ("Valves", "Prevent backflow of blood.")]),
("table", ["Component", "Role"], [["Plasma", "Carries food, CO<sub>2</sub>, nitrogenous wastes in dissolved form"], ["Red blood cells", "Haemoglobin carries O<sub>2</sub>"], ["Platelets", "Plug leaks by clotting at injured points"], ["Arteries", "Thick elastic walls, carry blood **away** from heart under pressure"], ["Veins", "Thin walls with valves, carry blood **to** heart"], ["Capillaries", "One cell thick; exchange of materials"], ["Lymph", "Colourless fluid from tissues; carries digested fat and drains excess fluid"]]),
("p", "Blood pressure is the force against vessel walls: normal about **120 mm Hg** systolic and **80 mm Hg** diastolic; high blood pressure can rupture vessels."),
("h3", "In plants"),
("table", ["Tissue", "Carries", "Process"], [["**Xylem**", "Water and minerals upward", "Root pressure at night; **transpiration pull** during the day pulls the water column"], ["**Phloem**", "Food (sucrose) in both directions", "**Translocation**: uses ATP energy; sugar loaded into phloem raises osmotic pressure and water enters"]]),
("h2", "5.5 Excretion"),
("note", "DEFINITION", "**Excretion** is the removal of harmful metabolic wastes from the body."),
("ul", ["**Human excretory system:** a pair of kidneys, two ureters, a urinary bladder and a urethra. Kidneys remove nitrogenous waste (urea, uric acid) from blood.", "**Nephron**, the unit of the kidney: a cluster of capillaries called **glomerulus** inside **Bowman's capsule**, plus a long tubule. Filtration at the glomerulus; useful glucose, amino acids and water are **reabsorbed**; the leftover is urine. A person produces 1.0 to 1.5 L daily.", "**Artificial kidney (haemodialysis)** cleans the blood of patients with kidney failure: blood flows through tubes bounded by a semi-permeable membrane in a dialysing fluid with no nitrogenous waste.", "**Plants:** excess water by transpiration, gases by stomata, wastes stored in leaves that fall, resins and gums in old xylem, and latex."]),
("h2", "Quick Revision: Likely Exam Questions"),
("qa", [("Why is the small intestine long and villi present?", "Greater area for digestion and absorption of food."), ("What is the role of bile?", "It makes the food alkaline and emulsifies fats."), ("Why do muscles cramp after heavy exercise?", "Anaerobic respiration forms lactic acid."), ("How are stomata opened and closed?", "Guard cells swell when water enters (open) and shrink when it leaves (close)."), ("Differentiate xylem and phloem.", "Xylem carries water and minerals upward; phloem carries food in both directions."), ("Why is double circulation needed?", "Oxygen-rich and oxygen-poor blood are kept separate for efficient supply of energy."), ("Name the functional unit of the kidney.", "Nephron."), ("Why is air in the lungs not completely emptied?", "A residual volume keeps alveoli open, so oxygen supply continues.")]),
],
takeaways=["**Photosynthesis:** 6CO<sub>2</sub> + 6H<sub>2</sub>O &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>12</sub>O<sub>6</sub> + 6O<sub>2</sub>; stomata control gas exchange.", "Human digestion: **amylase** (starch), **pepsin** (protein), **bile + lipase** (fat); absorption in **villi**.", "Respiration releases energy as **ATP**; anaerobic gives ethanol (yeast) or lactic acid (muscle).", "Heart: **four chambers, double circulation**; arteries away, veins towards the heart.", "Xylem moves water by **transpiration pull**; phloem moves food by **translocation**.", "**Nephron** filters blood; dialysis replaces kidney function."])
